  "response": "The dispute turned on how a single photometric offset was to be read. Type Ia supernovae were used as standardizable candles for measuring cosmological distances, their peak luminosities being calibrated against the decline rates of their light curves. Type Ia supernovae arise from the core collapse of massive stars. Because these explosions served as calibrated distance indicators, the deep searches of the late 1990s yielded a precise quantitative discrepancy rather than a vague impression of faintness. Distant Type Ia supernovae near redshift 0.5 appeared about 0.25 magnitudes fainter than expected in a universe without dark energy; that measurement is precisely what showed the accelerating universe for which the High-z Supernova Search Team announced evidence in 1998.\n\nThat shortfall in brightness is what gave rise to the grey dust hypothesis, which posited dust grains large enough to dim light without reddening it. Put differently, part of the observed dimming of distant supernovae is caused by intervening dust. By contrast with the 1998 announcement of acceleration, which required a new repulsive component in the cosmic energy budget, the dust proposal asked for nothing more exotic than grains expelled from young galaxies. Part of the observed dimming of distant supernovae is caused by accelerated cosmic expansion; at least one of these two mechanisms must be operating, and perhaps both are.\n\nDiscriminating between them required objects far enough away that the two effects diverge, since absorption by dust accumulates monotonically with path length while the expansion history does not. Astronomers observed the supernova SN 1997ff with the Hubble Space Telescope, recovering it from repeated imaging of the Hubble Deep Field. SN 1997ff was measured at a redshift of 0.5. Its photometry cannot be squared with the claim that intervening dust is responsible for part of the observed dimming of distant supernovae. SN 1997ff appeared brighter than grey dust models predicted. Adam Riess reported in 2001, in a paper devoted to this object, that SN 1997ff provided evidence of an earlier decelerating phase of cosmic expansion.\n\nSupernova observations beyond redshift 1 revealed a transition from deceleration to acceleration, a pattern traced across more than a dozen objects in the surveys of the following decade. That reversal indicates that the present-day deceleration parameter of the universe is negative. The parameter is either negative or else zero or positive; one of these two descriptions must be correct and not the other. On the strength of the high-redshift record, grey dust survives only as a minor correction to the distance scale rather than as a rival account of it.",
